# Quantum Shoebox: DIY & Open-Source Qubit

*A minimal, hackable open-source qubit you can build yourself.*
---
The **Quantum Shoebox** is a goal-driven, open-source project to build a minimal, room-temperature NV ensemble demonstrating the Zeeman effect and ODMR, with the longer-term goal of operation as a single qubit. The project will use affordable, off-the-shelf components — housed, quite literally, in a shoebox.

The shoebox represents physical constraints, cost discipline, and an explicit rejection of unnecessary infrastructure.

The project focuses on a **single logical qubit implemented using an NV ensemble**, designed for education, experimentation, and hands-on understanding of quantum hardware. This project does not attempt to produce new physics; the goal is to reproduce key NV-center experiments using minimal open hardware. It makes no claims of quantum advantage or performance, and instead emphasizes transparency, reproducibility, and learning by building.

## What this repository contains

Documents such as a bill of materials, STL files for the optical setup, and control software will be added as development progresses.

- [Product requirements document (PRD)](PRD.md)

## Scientific Background and Prior Work

The Quantum Shoebox builds on established research on nitrogen–vacancy (NV) centers in diamond and on prior educational and low-cost experimental implementations.

- M. W. Doherty, et al. "The nitrogen-vacancy colour centre in diamond." Physics Reports 528.1 (2013): 1-45. DOI:[10.1016/j.physrep.2013.02.001](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.physrep.2013.02.001)
- V. K. Sewani, et al. "Coherent control of NV− centers in diamond in a quantum teaching lab." American Journal of Physics 88.12 (2020): 1156-1169. DOI:[10.1119/10.0001905](https://doi.org/10.1119/10.0001905)
- G. Mariani, A. Umemoto, and S. Nomura. "A home-made portable device based on Arduino Uno for pulsed magnetic resonance of NV centers in diamond." AIP Advances 12.6 (2022). DOI:[10.1063/5.0089161](https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0089161)
- J. Stegemann, et al. "Modular low-cost 3D printed setup for experiments with NV centers in diamond." European Journal of Physics 44.3 (2023): 035402. DOI:[10.1088/1361-6404/acbe7c](https://doi.org/10.1088/1361-6404/acbe7c)
- N. Haverkamp, et al. "Low-cost ODMR experiments with nitrogen-vacancy centers in diamonds: a didactical approach to theory and experiment." EPJ Quantum Technology 12.1 (2025): 27. DOI:[10.1140/epjqt/s40507-025-00326-5](https://doi.org/10.1140/epjqt/s40507-025-00326-5)
- M. Carney, V. Kumaran. "Uncut Gem-An Open-Source Hackable Quantum Sensor." International Conference on Quantum Engineering Sciences and Technologies for Industry and Services (2025). arXiv:[2509.18329](https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.18329)
